  "tags": [
    {
      "name": "Authentication",
      "description": "Authentication endpoints for browser-based and device flows"
    },
    {
      "name": "Content",
      "description": "This endpoint is where you'll create and update your playlists, which you can then link to your \"Make Your Own\" cards."
    },
    {
      "name": "Devices"
    },
    {
      "name": "Family",
      "description": "This lets you perform actions that affect your family's card library"
    },
    {
      "name": "Family Library Groups",
      "description": "A family library group is a named collection of content that you may use to organise content in your library."
    },
    {
      "name": "Icons",
      "description": "A a full set of RESTful endpoints to manage and serve icons"
    },
    {
      "name": "Media",
      "description": "### Why do we transcode uploaded audio files?\n\n- To balance quality vs compression + size for the players\n    \n- To convert everything into a supported format and codec for players\n    \n- To correct the `moov` atom which can affect the players ability to stream the audio\n    \n\n### What do we transcode audio files to?\n\n- We use [FFmpeg](https://ffmpeg.org/) for all transcoding\n    \n- MP4 format, encoded with AAC codec (`libfdk_aac`)\n    \n- Bit Rate - 128Kbps (FFmpeg default)\n    \n- Sample Rate\n    - We support the following sample rates - 11,025kHz, 22,050kHz, 44,100kHz, 48,000kHz\n    - If an uploaded audio file doesn’t match one of those sample rates, we re-sample it to 44,100kHz\n- Channels - we do not modify the channels (mono, stereo etc…). Whatever the uploaded audio file has, we respect.\n    \n\n### Is there a file size limit for uploaded audio files?\n\n- We apply an arbitary limit of 1Gb file size to reduce inconvenience to users. But still retain a limit to reduce issues with large transcodes (long transcodes can timeout or corrupt)\n- Not all uploaded audio files are equal. A 500Mb uncompressed audio file (e.g. \\*.flac) can process much faster than a 100Mb highly compressed file. This can impact the time and success rate of a transcode."
    }
  ]
